#version 460

// ===========================================================================
// WAVEFRONT SCATTER -- THE REPLAY INSTANTIATION
// ===========================================================================
//
// The SECOND instantiation of shaders/includes/diff/traverse.glsl. Its
// sibling shaders/diff/wf_scatter.comp is the first. Everything the scatter
// stage does is in that one include -- extensions, bindings, the Push block,
// the local size, the constants, the RNG reconstruction and fast-forward, the
// BSDF, the environment sample, next-event estimation and MIS, the path
// advance and the re-queue. Neither file adds anything to it except the body
// of `diffVertexHook` and a `main` that calls `diffTraverse()`.
//
// WHAT "REPLAY" MEANS HERE, AND WHAT IT IS NOT.
//
// This stage is dispatched in the SAME position of the SAME
// ohao::diff::WavefrontLoop that the forward stage occupies, in a second,
// independent run of the whole loop -- generate, then [intersect, scatter] x
// bounces -- from re-zeroed buffers with the SAME iteration seed. It is not
// handed the forward run's path, its RNG stream, or any record of what the
// forward run did. It re-derives all of it, which it can only do because the
// seed invariant holds (spec section 4.5): a path is a pure function of
// (pixel, sampleIndex, iterationSeed), every stage reconstructs its RNG from
// (pixelIndex, sampleIndex, iterationSeed) and fast-forwards by the `bounce`
// it reads out of path state, and nothing survives in a register across a
// dispatch boundary.
//
// That is exactly the property path-replay backpropagation rests on, and it
// is why this task exists BEFORE any adjoint does. If the replayed path
// diverges from the forward path by so much as one RNG draw, every gradient
// computed on it is silently wrong -- no crash, no NaN, no diagnostic -- and
// found later it would look like a bad df/dtheta derivation rather than like
// a stream that went out of step. `diff_gpu_probe.cpp`'s replay-equivalence
// check compares this stage's binding-3 vertex trace against the forward
// stage's, bit for bit, for every path and every bounce: the five draws, the
// draw count, and the origin, direction, throughput and hit distance each
// traversal read out of path state.
//
// WHY THE HOOK IS EMPTY.
//
// Stage 1 Task 1 adds NO adjoint, NO gradient and NO arena write, on purpose:
// the point of this task is to establish that two instantiations walk one
// path, while there is still nothing else in the picture to confuse the
// diagnosis with. The whole of the difference between the forward pass and
// the replay pass is therefore this function's body -- the forward one
// accumulates the film, this one does nothing -- and that difference is the
// only place a later task has to put the PRB recursion:
//
//     dL/dtheta  +=  v.adjoint * (df/dtheta) * v.Li / v.pdf
//     v.adjoint   =  v.adjoint * v.bsdfWeight
//
// Every factor those two lines need is already a field of `DiffVertex` (see
// traverse.glsl), which is why the signature could be fixed here, one task
// before the first consumer of it exists.
//
// NOTE THAT THIS STAGE STILL WRITES PATH STATE AND STILL RE-QUEUES. That is
// not an oversight and it is not "the forward pass again": it is what walking
// the path MEANS in a wavefront design, where the path advances across
// dispatch boundaries through path state rather than through registers. The
// replay run's path state is its own -- it re-zeroes and re-generates -- so
// it cannot disturb the forward run it is being compared against.
//
// The film (binding 9) is declared by traverse.glsl and is never written by
// this stage, because this hook does not write it. The descriptor set still
// binds a buffer there; the probe binds this stage its OWN film so that a
// future hook that does touch it cannot contaminate the forward film it is
// compared against.

#include "diff/traverse.glsl"

// THE REPLAY HOOK. Deliberately empty -- see "WHY THE HOOK IS EMPTY" above.
// `v` is unnamed-but-used in the sense that matters: traverse.glsl fills
// every field of it before this call, and the replay-equivalence check
// verifies that those fields were reached by the identical traversal. An
// empty body is what makes this task's claim narrow and checkable: whatever
// the check finds, it cannot be an artefact of something this file did at the
// vertex, because this file does nothing at the vertex.
void diffVertexHook(inout DiffVertex v) {}

void main() { diffTraverse(); }
